    "set5-l2-q04-05": {
      "scriptHash": "9a55feb100d0a467317ff1480924806bcc854553556099a65ebc9754dcc969c3",
      "sigVersion": 3,
      "bytes": 305772,
      "durationSec": 28.86,
      "audioSha256": "eb571acff2d0ee169d41a4c0aae1111993b99fcd783be4ddeb1a7ceafc21983f",
      "stt": {
        "verdict": "PASS",
        "wer": 0.0,
        "backend": "faster_whisper",
        "reason": "WER 0.0%",
        "transcript": "Professor Adams, I wanted to update you on my dissertation progress. I've completed the data analysis chapter. That's great news. How did the statistical models turn out? Better than expected, actually. The results support my hypothesis pretty strongly. I just need to write up the discussion section now. Excellent. Once that's done, send me the full draft. I'll need at least two weeks to review it before we schedule your defense. Understood. I'm hoping to have everything for you by the end of this month.",
        "audioSha256": "eb571acff2d0ee169d41a4c0aae1111993b99fcd783be4ddeb1a7ceafc21983f",
        "scriptHash": "9a55feb100d0a467317ff1480924806bcc854553556099a65ebc9754dcc969c3"
      }
    },
    "set5-l2-q06-07": {
      "scriptHash": "aa93e0d6f76fba2ade2157b5f584893e6cad38cf05e322782958e92f00c3b9c5",
      "sigVersion": 3,
      "bytes": 276024,
      "durationSec": 26.45,
      "audioSha256": "b17127e9a21cc899e0fbd87df5eb94f0f19f7a35a1b8ded91efbea90becd9cc4",
      "stt": {
        "verdict": "PASS",
        "wer": 0.0,
        "backend": "faster_whisper",
        "reason": "WER 0.0%",
        "transcript": "Did you see the email about the leadership conference next week? Yes, the one about the keynote speaker change. I was looking forward to hearing Dr. Chen speak about innovation in education. Same here, but the replacement speaker is actually quite well known in organizational psychology. She wrote that book on team dynamics. Oh, I've heard of her. That might be even more relevant to my work in human resources, actually. Right, I thought you'd find it interesting.",
        "audioSha256": "b17127e9a21cc899e0fbd87df5eb94f0f19f7a35a1b8ded91efbea90becd9cc4",
        "scriptHash": "aa93e0d6f76fba2ade2157b5f584893e6cad38cf05e322782958e92f00c3b9c5"
      }
    },
    "set5-l2-q08-11": {
      "scriptHash": "95dd167e018270b63af844b727389bcf5b25eac6c579ef296e1df0b32f9d201e",
      "sigVersion": 3,
      "bytes": 971810,
      "durationSec": 95.41,
      "audioSha256": "2f1fd885d5248f840cb87ed712c0f9dd56cdd825b34a2702d72234b0780dcca8",
      "stt": {
        "verdict": "PASS",
        "wer": 0.0084,
        "backend": "faster_whisper",
        "reason": "WER 0.8%",
        "transcript": "Alright, today I want to compare two different strategies animals use to regulate their body temperature, being warm-blooded versus cold-blooded. These terms are a bit simplified, but they help us understand some fundamental differences. Warm-blooded animals, which include mammals and birds, generate their own body heat internally. Their metabolism is constantly working to maintain a stable internal temperature, regardless of the environment. A human body stays around 37 degrees Celsius, whether you're in a hot desert or a cold mountain. This gives warm-blooded animals a significant advantage. They can remain active in a wide range of conditions. Cold-blooded animals, like reptiles, fish and insects, work differently. They rely on external sources to regulate their temperature. A lizard, for instance, will bask in the sun to warm up and seek shade when it gets too hot. Their body temperature changes with their surroundings. Now each strategy has trade-offs. Warm-blooded animals can be active any time, but they need to eat a lot more food to fuel their metabolism. A mouse might need to eat half its body weight daily. Cold-blooded animals need much less food, but their activity is limited by environmental conditions. On cold days they become sluggish and slow. Neither approach is superior. They're just different solutions to the same problem of surviving in various environments. Next, we will examine how some animals can actually switch between these strategies.",
        "audioSha256": "2f1fd885d5248f840cb87ed712c0f9dd56cdd825b34a2702d72234b0780dcca8",
        "scriptHash": "95dd167e018270b63af844b727389bcf5b25eac6c579ef296e1df0b32f9d201e"
      }
    },
    "set5-l2-q12-15": {
      "scriptHash": "4f31b41df316a2ddd039f2f0ea0186cc9095d6f4cb13757c9afca27d066e153e",
      "sigVersion": 3,
      "bytes": 738440,
      "durationSec": 88.54,
      "audioSha256": "843364b4366951280abdad2ad8eda3c465ee895f2ca26ded8dbf98f3a530c9d4",
      "stt": {
        "verdict": "PASS",
        "wer": 0.0086,
        "backend": "faster_whisper",
        "reason": "WER 0.9%",
        "transcript": "Okay, so let's talk about something you encounter every day but might not think much about, the states of matter. Most of you know there are three main states, solid, liquid, and gas. But do you understand what's actually happening at the molecular level? In a solid, molecules are packed tightly together in fixed positions. They vibrate but don't move around freely. This is why solids maintain their shape, put a rock on a table, and it stays exactly where you put it. The molecules are locked in place. Liquids are different. The molecules are still closed together but they can slide past one another. This is why liquids take the shape of their container. Pour water into a glass and it conforms to the glass's shape. The molecules have more freedom to move but are still attracted to each other. In gases, molecules have the most energy and freedom. They spread out to fill whatever space is available and move rapidly in all directions. The molecules are far apart and barely interact with each other. This is why gases can be compressed easily. There's lots of empty space between molecules. What determines which state matter is in? Temperature and pressure. Add heat energy and molecules move faster. Eventually, a solid becomes liquid and a liquid becomes gas. Remove heat and the opposite happens. It's all about how much energy the molecules have.",
        "audioSha256": "843364b4366951280abdad2ad8eda3c465ee895f2ca26ded8dbf98f3a530c9d4",
        "scriptHash": "4f31b41df316a2ddd039f2f0ea0186cc9095d6f4cb13757c9afca27d066e153e"
      }
    },
    "set5-s1-instructions": {
